<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capernaum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE–11th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Kfar Naḥum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Capernaum was a fishing village on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, established under the Hasmoneans in the 2nd century BCE, with a population of about 1,500 in the 1st century CE. All four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> name it, and the Synoptic Gospels present it as the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>' public ministry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, where he taught in the synagogue and healed. Excavations have found two synagogues built in succession on the same spot and a house turned into a church over the 4th and 5th centuries, which Christian tradition holds was the home of Saint Peter. The village was inhabited until the 11th century and abandoned before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capernaum.jpg" alt="Capernaum" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eddie Gerald · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sites_of_Christianity_in_the_Galillee_-_Ruins_of_the_ancient_Great_Synagogue_at_Capernaum_(or_Kfar_Nahum)_on_the_shore_of_the_Lake_of_Galilee,_Northern_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
